## Part 1: The EuroShop Cost Model
I compared three AI tiers for handling 30,000 customer service emails/month (200 input / 400 output tokens each):
| Metric | Budget | Standard | Premium |
|---|---|---|---|
| Token cost/month | $300.00 | $900.00 | $1,800.00 |
| Error cost/month | €45,000.00 | €22,500.00 | €7,500.00 |
| Cost per email | $1.51 | $0.78 | $0.31 |
| Cost per *correctly handled* email | $2.16 | $0.92 | $0.33 |

**Recommendation:** Premium. Its token cost is highest, but the 95% success rate cuts human-correction costs so sharply that its cost per correctly handled email ($0.33) beats both other tiers. The Budget tier looks cheap on paper but is actually the most expensive system overall, because its 30% error rate generates human-fix costs that dwarf the token savings.

**If presenting to the CFO:** lead with *total cost per email*, not token cost. Token cost alone is misleading — human error correction is over 95% of real spend in the Budget case, so the "cheapest" tier by token price is the most expensive in practice.

*(Note on my own working: I initially mixed USD and EUR in the annual cost totals without converting — a reminder that the token cost and error cost lines are in different currencies and need to be reconciled before adding.)*
## Part 2: Token Count Table — Multilingual Comparison

Calculated at $0.03 per 1,000 input tokens, English vs. French:

| Document | English Tokens | English Cost | French Tokens | French Cost | Difference | % Increase |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Doc 1 | 149 | $0.00447 | 186 | $0.00558 | $0.00111 | 24.83% |
| Doc 2 | 98 | $0.00294 | 151 | $0.00453 | $0.00159 | 54.08% |
| Doc 3 | 164 | $0.00492 | 225 | $0.00675 | $0.00183 | 37.20% |

**Biggest increase:** Doc 2, at 54.08% more tokens in French than English.
**Trade-off question — should a customer service chatbot force English to cut costs?**
Given a 54% cost increase for French isn't a small margin — it's more than half again the cost of the English version. Forcing customers into English to save on tokens would trade a real, ongoing cost saving against a real, ongoing service quality cost: customers who aren't fluent in English get worse support, which risks churn, complaints, and brand damage that likely outweighs the token savings.
